  "notes": "Disco/electro-pop producer and remixer, and one of the fully documented entries in this section — aliases, remix philosophy, arrangement method, specific gear on named records and dated credits are all sourced. The era is 1996—present per the research; the entry is written on the 2010s—2020s work, which is where the parameters come from. The drums are his: \"four-on-the-floor kick with claps on 2&4, offbeat disco hats opening to 16ths, and cowbell/tambourine as period markers\", so the kick anchors all four beats, `hihat.base` is weighted across 8th and 16th, `openHat.prob` is 0.66 on all four offbeats, and `cowbell` and `tambourine` lead the perc lanes. The one thing that separates him from every other four-on-the-floor model in this roster is documented as his contribution to other people's records: \"he adds bounce — i.e. groove displacement and swing on the hats — to other producers' skeletons.\" That is `swing.amount` 0.56 on a 16th grid over an otherwise machine-tight quantize, which is a combination no other model here authors. The bass is the second documented contribution: \"no 808. His documented contribution is bass-groove development — a syncopated, played-feeling synth or electric bass line that carries the record\", so the bassline is `independent_riff` at `followRootsProb` 0.3 with a full passing-tone set and the highest `anticipationProb` on the producer side, and no `bass808` block is authored. Harmony is \"minor-mode disco vamps with 7ths and a high sustained drone synth used as a disco trope, plus analogue string voicings in the pre-chorus\", so `triad` is low, `harmonicRhythm` is vamp-led and the counter carries both the drone (`sustain_pad`) and the arpeggio. The arrangement is his stated method: \"the climax is achieved by unmuting layers rather than by a drop\", which is why `dropOutBeats` is low and the structures are long and additive rather than terraced. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability, `swing.amount` 0.56 (\"bounce\" is a direction), the humanize pair and `bpm.mode` 119 (the midpoint of the stated 110—128 production band).",
